# Marimba A4, rosewood table and Table I rosewood, cut solved in the engine, no box, printed rubber mallet.
name	marimba-a4-rosewood-nobox-rubber
source	Bar outline: J. La Favre, lafavre.us/marimba-bars.htm, table of an unnamed commercial 5-octave marimba with rosewood bars: A row, length 12 15/16 in, width 1 7/8 in, thickness 0.93 in. Wood: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544, Rosewood bar: E 2.13e10, density 1015, eta 2.35e-7 s, gamma_B 51.58 1/s, through the packs named below (E and density are read from the pack of Doutaut and Chaigne 1994, which prints the same values). Ratios: USDA Wood Handbook FPL-GTR-282, Honduras mahogany, stand-in. Mallet: the same Table I, Korogi No. 4 rubber, K 3.7e7, 23.6 g, measured on a padouk bar; 1.0 m/s and strike 15.9 cm of 29.3 cm as the paper's Fig. 8.
note	NOT in the source: the undercut (solved here in the engine, modes only, a circular arc, half reach 63.315 mm, middle 9.718 mm, so f1 sits at equal temperament and f2 = 4 f1, from the builder's statement for its own padouk bars on the same page; the rosewood table prints no arch length and no tuning). Strike fraction carried from the xylophone bar; no marimba strike is printed. Not the recording's bar or mallet: Iowa states no bar wood and no bar outline; the recording's file names a cord mallet or a roll, and a Korogi rubber xylophone mallet is not that mallet. No resonator: a marimba has a tube under each bar, this render has none.
